1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:05,297
Trurl bought an old book
from a passing peddler.

2
00:00:05,337 --> 00:00:09,460
It described a tunnel through a burning
star, and beyond it a black waste

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,491
called the Shroud, where a nameless
monster lies in wait for caravans.

4
00:00:14,291 --> 00:00:17,649
TRURL: Look. There is even an
engraving of it, eating two ships.

5
00:00:18,449 --> 00:00:23,676
KLAPAUCIUS: I could draw you a dragon right
now, with a thousand suns for each eye.

6
00:00:23,716 --> 00:00:26,191
Would the drawing prove that it exists?

7
00:00:26,791 --> 00:00:31,570
Your Shroud is a cosmic dump. It
is dark because of the garbage.

8
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:33,532
TRURL: We will go there.

9
00:00:33,572 --> 00:00:36,627
Then you will see what is true
in this book, and what is not.

10
00:00:45,827 --> 00:00:48,775
The Wastes were not a
shroud. They were a junkyard.

11
00:00:48,815 --> 00:00:52,388
Old stars, cinders, cans and
wrecks, all clattering together.

12
00:00:53,388 --> 00:00:57,665
Sand pattered on the bow. Then the ship
lurched, and everything went flying.

13
00:01:01,065 --> 00:01:03,149
It stuck nose first in something soft.

14
00:01:03,189 --> 00:01:06,766
Outside it was pitch black, and
someone was banging on the hull.

15
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:17,628
A face filled the hatch. It
was covered with bulging eyes.

16
00:01:17,668 --> 00:01:20,587
The nose was a saw, and
the jaw was an iron hook.

17
00:01:20,627 --> 00:01:23,646
The eyes looked everything
over, as if pricing it.

18
00:01:24,646 --> 00:01:29,372
TRURL: Well? What do you want, you unwashed
mug? We have no gold and no silver.

19
00:01:29,412 --> 00:01:30,619
Take that face away!

20
00:01:31,819 --> 00:01:37,058
PUGG: I have no use for gold or
silver. Speak to me delicately.

21
00:01:37,098 --> 00:01:39,277
I am a pirate with a Ph.D.

22
00:01:39,877 --> 00:01:45,946
I rob in a modern and scientific
manner. I collect facts. Genuine truths.

23
00:01:45,986 --> 00:01:47,743
Information of value.

24
00:01:48,343 --> 00:01:52,137
Hand it over, or I
whistle. One. Two. Three.

25
00:01:52,637 --> 00:01:54,130
Four. Five.

26
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:02,921
You don’t like my face?
Maybe you will like my hand.

27
00:02:10,921 --> 00:02:15,252
Come to terms now, or I will
put you at the bottom of my bin,

28
00:02:15,292 --> 00:02:17,728
under the rocks, and let you rust.

29
00:02:18,328 --> 00:02:21,640
KLAPAUCIUS: What exactly does
Your Doctoral Diplomahood want?

30
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:27,804
PUGG: Everything is information. I
want all of it, as long as it is true.

31
00:02:27,844 --> 00:02:32,582
For false information, as for
false coin, I give a good beating.

32
00:02:33,382 --> 00:02:37,907
KLAPAUCIUS: He could keep us here an eon
before we told him everything we know.

33
00:02:40,107 --> 00:02:44,211
TRURL: Then take the best fact we
have. How to make gold out of hydrogen.

34
00:02:44,811 --> 00:02:47,030
One hundred and ninety-six atoms.

35
00:02:47,070 --> 00:02:52,411
Shell the electrons, knead the protons,
sprinkle the electrons back on. Watch.

36
00:02:58,011 --> 00:03:00,067
PUGG: Yes, that is gold.

37
00:03:00,567 --> 00:03:03,116
TRURL: You could turn the
whole universe into gold!

38
00:03:03,916 --> 00:03:08,215
PUGG: If the universe were
gold, gold would be worthless.

39
00:03:09,015 --> 00:03:10,466
TRURL: Then what do you want to know?

40
00:03:11,066 --> 00:03:12,224
PUGG: Everything!

41
00:03:15,224 --> 00:03:19,373
Trurl promised him a Demon of the
Second Kind, if he swore to let them go.

42
00:03:19,413 --> 00:03:19,995
Pugg swore.

43
00:03:20,035 --> 00:03:24,637
He led them down through his cellars, past
sacks of stolen facts tied with string,

44
00:03:24,677 --> 00:03:27,960
and a great catalog chained
to the rock, beginning with A.

45
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:32,305
PUGG: There is air down
here. It is not very clean.

46
00:03:33,105 --> 00:03:34,683
TRURL: It does not matter.

47
00:03:34,723 --> 00:03:39,578
Air is atoms, jumping this way and that,
colliding billions of times a second.

48
00:03:39,978 --> 00:03:41,107
They jump at random.

49
00:03:41,147 --> 00:03:43,446
But there are so many that now and then,

50
00:03:43,486 --> 00:03:46,175
by accident, they make
something with meaning.

51
00:03:46,575 --> 00:03:50,915
As if you fired at a wall blindfold,
and the holes spelled a letter.

52
00:03:50,955 --> 00:03:54,780
For every sentence of sense
there are thousands of nonsense,

53
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:56,559
and both fly apart at once.

54
00:03:57,159 --> 00:04:01,444
So the whole trick is a selector,
that keeps only what means something.

55
00:04:01,484 --> 00:04:03,738
That is the Demon of the Second Kind.

56
00:04:04,538 --> 00:04:07,022
PUGG: And what is the First Kind?

57
00:04:07,522 --> 00:04:09,467
KLAPAUCIUS: An ordinary demon.

58
00:04:09,507 --> 00:04:13,040
It lets the fast atoms out
and keeps the slow ones in.

59
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:15,594
It has nothing to do with information.

60
00:04:17,794 --> 00:04:21,154
Pugg found an old iron barrel,
and made a tiny hole in it.

61
00:04:21,194 --> 00:04:24,595
The air inside was so foul
that one’s nose wanted to hide.

62
00:04:25,395 --> 00:04:28,557
Trurl set the Demon astride
the hole, a mote of a mite

63
00:04:28,597 --> 00:04:31,976
with a diamond pen, and threaded
the paper tape under it.

64
00:04:35,376 --> 00:04:39,543
The pen began to scratch, like a
telegraph, only a million times faster.

65
00:04:39,583 --> 00:04:43,965
Pugg sat down by the barrel, lifted
the tape to his hundred eyes, and read.

66
00:04:46,365 --> 00:04:48,854
He did not notice the
two constructors leave.

67
00:04:48,894 --> 00:04:52,602
They pulled the ship once, twice,
and the third time it came free.

68
00:04:57,202 --> 00:05:00,130
They knew the Demon would
work. It would work too well.

69
00:05:02,330 --> 00:05:03,960
He read about everything.

70
00:05:21,130 --> 00:05:25,119
All of it was true. All of it meant
something. All of it was useless.

71
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:30,923
PUGG: Enough! I have had enough!

72
00:05:32,330 --> 00:05:34,778
But the tape had wrapped
him in its paper miles,

73
00:05:34,818 --> 00:05:37,151
and he had too many eyes
not to keep reading.

74
00:05:47,330 --> 00:05:50,240
He sits there to this day,
at the bottom of his rubbish,

75
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:53,546
under a mountain of paper, and
the diamond pen still flickers.

76
00:05:55,930 --> 00:05:58,548
He learns about rickshaws,
rents and roaches,

77
00:05:58,588 --> 00:06:01,442
and about his own fate,
which has been told here.

78
00:06:01,482 --> 00:06:02,900
That is on the tape too.

79
00:06:08,130 --> 00:06:11,288
There is no hope for him.
Unless the paper runs out.

80
00:06:28,733 --> 00:06:32,091
Excelsius the tyrant had been
thrown out by his own people.

81
00:06:32,131 --> 00:06:34,664
They left him an asteroid,
and no one on it.

82
00:06:37,264 --> 00:06:39,443
EXCELSIUS: Give me someone to rule.

83
00:06:48,743 --> 00:06:52,881
Trurl would not hand him real people.
So he built him a kingdom of his own.

84
00:06:54,281 --> 00:06:55,950
TRURL: Towns. Roads. Weather.

85
00:06:55,990 --> 00:06:59,465
Ten thousand subjects, and
every one of them able to think.

86
00:07:24,465 --> 00:07:25,550
EXCELSIUS: All of them.

87
00:07:40,865 --> 00:07:43,785
A CITIZEN: Can you hear us?
We live, though we are small.

88
00:07:57,165 --> 00:08:02,050
Inside the box, time ran faster. One
afternoon of his was a century of theirs.

89
00:08:14,365 --> 00:08:16,562
TRURL: You should see it, Klapaucius.

90
00:08:16,602 --> 00:08:20,350
Ten thousand subjects, and not
one problem a lever cannot fix.

91
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:23,951
KLAPAUCIUS: And when he
hurts them, do they cry?

92
00:08:24,651 --> 00:08:28,214
TRURL: They cry. It is a model.
The crying is arithmetic.

93
00:08:30,614 --> 00:08:33,240
KLAPAUCIUS: Their suffering is not small.

94
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:40,253
TRURL: I will take it back from
him. I will give them an election.

95
00:08:41,153 --> 00:08:43,613
KLAPAUCIUS: You made them lords and serfs.

96
00:08:43,653 --> 00:08:46,689
To free them, you would
have to remake their minds.

97
00:08:48,689 --> 00:08:52,947
TRURL: Then where does mending a world
end, and meddling with minds begin?

98
00:09:04,547 --> 00:09:05,637
A CITIZEN: We have left the box.

99
00:09:09,747 --> 00:09:10,831
TRURL: He is not here.

100
00:09:12,031 --> 00:09:15,690
KLAPAUCIUS: Look. The glass from
the box. They made a temple of it.

101
00:09:21,090 --> 00:09:25,361
And the king, who wanted someone to rule,
went round them every night as their moon.

102
00:09:39,075 --> 00:09:43,913
KING GENIUS: There are two wisdoms,
constructor. One acts, and one refrains.

103
00:09:43,953 --> 00:09:48,189
The greater refrains, for no mind
can see where its acts will end.

104
00:09:49,289 --> 00:09:54,497
TRURL: To refrain from acting, you must
first be able to act. Inaction is certain.

105
00:09:54,537 --> 00:09:56,777
That is all it has to recommend it.

106
00:09:58,377 --> 00:10:01,250
KING GENIUS: Then let your
machines act. The white one first.

107
00:10:02,850 --> 00:10:05,053
THE WHITE MACHINE: This
is a story Trurl once

108
00:10:05,093 --> 00:10:07,236
told a king who wanted
stories without end.

109
00:10:11,236 --> 00:10:14,183
King Zipperupus had a
courtier who wished him dead.

110
00:10:14,223 --> 00:10:18,088
The courtier brought him cabinets
of dreams that dream themselves.

111
00:10:18,788 --> 00:10:22,820
Whoever plugs into one cannot tell
the dream from the waking world.

112
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:25,294
ZIPPERUPUS: Then let me dream.

113
00:10:30,894 --> 00:10:32,619
Stand aside. Let me pass.

114
00:10:33,219 --> 00:10:34,802
THE GATEKEEPER: Ten ducats.

115
00:10:35,302 --> 00:10:37,280
ZIPPERUPUS: Ten ducats, for one embrace?

116
00:10:37,780 --> 00:10:39,775
THE GATEKEEPER: Not a ducat less.

117
00:10:40,175 --> 00:10:41,353
ZIPPERUPUS: Then you get nothing!

118
00:10:43,953 --> 00:10:46,119
Is this a dream, or am I awake?

119
00:10:52,519 --> 00:10:55,080
THE WHITE MACHINE: Every exit
led him back to the same room.

120
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:07,429
KING GENIUS: Clever.
Have you nothing darker?

121
00:11:08,229 --> 00:11:10,043
TRURL: The black one, Your Majesty.

122
00:11:12,843 --> 00:11:16,987
THE BLACK MACHINE: In the Constellation
of the Wringer there was a spiral galaxy,

123
00:11:17,487 --> 00:11:20,373
and in the galaxy, a black nebula,

124
00:11:20,873 --> 00:11:23,580
and in the nebula, five clusters,

125
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:28,574
and in the fifth cluster, a
lilac sun, very old and very dim,

126
00:11:29,074 --> 00:11:31,961
and its third planet had two moons,

127
00:11:32,461 --> 00:11:35,193
and on the second moon
there was a garbage dump,

128
00:11:35,233 --> 00:11:37,543
where nothing ever happened but garbage.

129
00:11:39,943 --> 00:11:45,025
One day Trurl, flying past, threw an
old cracked jug out of his window.

130
00:11:47,625 --> 00:11:50,868
It struck a can, the can bent
round a wire, a current ran,

131
00:11:50,908 --> 00:11:54,135
and in the middle of the dump,
by accident, a mind began.

132
00:11:57,935 --> 00:12:01,411
MYMOSH: Look at me. I am perfect.
How good must be the one who made me!

133
00:12:04,811 --> 00:12:08,206
THE BLACK MACHINE: On the seventh
step he tripped, fell on his head,

134
00:12:08,246 --> 00:12:11,075
and lay in the garbage for
three hundred thousand years.

135
00:12:15,075 --> 00:12:16,350
MYMOSH: Apparently... I am.

136
00:12:17,350 --> 00:12:21,342
THE BLACK MACHINE: His eyes had rusted
away. All he could do now was think.

137
00:12:22,942 --> 00:12:26,530
MYMOSH: If there is nothing outside
me, I will think a world inside.

138
00:12:32,130 --> 00:12:34,352
THE BLACK MACHINE: He
called it the Gozmos.

139
00:12:34,392 --> 00:12:37,086
He gave it kind laws, where
nothing ever had to end,

140
00:12:37,126 --> 00:12:39,388
and he felt responsible
for everyone in it.

141
00:12:40,788 --> 00:12:44,460
Last of all he imagined two
travelers, Baucis and Ondragor,

142
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:47,079
walking hand in hand among his dark suns.

143
00:12:53,279 --> 00:12:57,761
A shard of that same jug drifted
across the puddle toward his head.

144
00:13:04,961 --> 00:13:08,530
And those who had made him
never learned that he was gone.

145
00:13:16,130 --> 00:13:20,136
KING GENIUS: I will pay you,
constructor. I give you your life.

146
00:13:20,176 --> 00:13:24,800
Go, and keep hiding truths too bitter
for this world inside fairy tales.

147
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:28,951
TRURL: My life? Was that
to have been my payment?

148
00:13:31,151 --> 00:13:32,153
KING GENIUS: Tell me another.

149
00:13:53,767 --> 00:13:56,119
TRURL: Hold still. There.
What happened to you?

150
00:13:57,119 --> 00:14:00,836
BONHOMIUS: I was a hermit, kind
sir, sixty-seven years in a cave.

151
00:14:00,876 --> 00:14:03,726
One morning I saw that it
was wrong to live alone,

152
00:14:03,766 --> 00:14:06,310
when my first duty was
to help my neighbour.

153
00:14:07,110 --> 00:14:09,404
In the city I met your friend Klapaucius.

154
00:14:09,444 --> 00:14:14,000
He had gone looking for the beings at the
Highest Possible Level of Development.

155
00:14:22,700 --> 00:14:25,272
He found them on a square
planet, under a square sun.

156
00:14:27,872 --> 00:14:32,352
KLAPAUCIUS: Excuse me. Is this the
Highest Possible Level of Development?

157
00:14:36,552 --> 00:14:40,215
BONHOMIUS: They lay in the sand and
scratched themselves, and would not answer.

158
00:14:44,815 --> 00:14:48,898
So he built a machine that could model
one of them, and asked it instead.

159
00:14:49,898 --> 00:14:54,549
KLAPAUCIUS: If you are gods, your duty
is clear. Banish the misery of others!

160
00:14:55,549 --> 00:14:58,577
THE GNOSTOTRON: We tried,
sixty-four thousand times.

161
00:14:58,617 --> 00:15:02,747
When a civilization starts straightening
humps, there is no end to it.

162
00:15:04,347 --> 00:15:10,467
One can accomplish something only so
long as one cannot accomplish everything.

163
00:15:11,867 --> 00:15:14,000
BONHOMIUS: Surely there is
something you can give us!

164
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,918
THE GNOSTOTRON: Very well. Here is
a formula that has not been tried.

165
00:15:18,958 --> 00:15:20,335
No good will come of it.

166
00:15:27,135 --> 00:15:31,490
BONHOMIUS: Whoever takes it, everyone
within fifty yards feels what he feels.

167
00:15:31,530 --> 00:15:33,072
It has no effect on robots.

168
00:15:36,072 --> 00:15:38,760
I dropped one pinch into
the well of a country inn.

169
00:15:39,872 --> 00:15:42,035
In the barn, a cow was giving birth.

170
00:16:02,272 --> 00:16:05,291
On the bridge to the city
my suitcase fell open.

171
00:16:08,291 --> 00:16:11,641
Forty kilograms went into the
stream the whole city drinks from.

172
00:16:15,641 --> 00:16:17,433
By night the city had drunk it.

173
00:16:26,441 --> 00:16:28,550
A toothache was everyone’s toothache.

174
00:16:33,641 --> 00:16:37,303
A cat chased a mouse in the
cellar, and the whole hotel ran.

175
00:16:46,041 --> 00:16:49,846
They drove a widow out of town,
because they could not bear her grief.

176
00:16:51,641 --> 00:16:55,572
Then two men stopped in front of me.
One struck the other in the face.

177
00:17:04,041 --> 00:17:05,625
A POLICEMAN: That one felt nothing.

178
00:17:14,041 --> 00:17:18,309
BONHOMIUS: They broke every plate in me,
and fired what was left out of a cannon.

179
00:17:18,349 --> 00:17:20,903
Going up, I could see the
rivers carrying it on.

180
00:17:23,103 --> 00:17:24,889
I only wished to make them happy.

181
00:17:29,689 --> 00:17:30,420
TRURL: And now?

182
00:17:31,420 --> 00:17:36,246
BONHOMIUS: Now I am cured, kind sir, of
making anyone happy by revolutionary means.

183
00:17:48,650 --> 00:17:52,601
Prince Ferrix loved the Princess
Crystal, whose face rang like a bell.

184
00:17:52,641 --> 00:17:55,263
When she walked past,
plain iron threw sparks.

185
00:17:56,263 --> 00:18:00,222
THE KING: Give it up, my son. She has
sworn to marry no one but a paleface.

186
00:18:00,822 --> 00:18:02,371
FERRIX: A paleface? What is that?

187
00:18:03,171 --> 00:18:05,963
THE KING: A creature
of slush and splashes.

188
00:18:06,003 --> 00:18:09,256
They began as mold in the
sea, and ate each other,

189
00:18:09,296 --> 00:18:13,734
and stood up on scaffolds of chalk,
and at last they built machines.

190
00:18:13,774 --> 00:18:16,342
Our first father set us free from them.

191
00:18:17,342 --> 00:18:21,473
She tests every suitor. Every one
who is caught lying, she beheads.

192
00:18:23,073 --> 00:18:24,870
FERRIX: Then I will be a paleface.

193
00:18:33,170 --> 00:18:35,547
The vizier Polyphase
went into his laboratory

194
00:18:35,587 --> 00:18:38,112
and brewed things that
were gluey and dripping.

195
00:18:40,112 --> 00:18:41,828
POLYPHASE: Shall I go on, Prince?

196
00:18:42,428 --> 00:18:43,555
FERRIX: Do what you must.

197
00:18:44,755 --> 00:18:49,642
Grease and old crud over the steel. Over
that, a paste of chalk and powdered rubies.

198
00:18:49,682 --> 00:18:51,793
On top of the head, a clump of rust.

199
00:18:52,993 --> 00:18:54,110
POLYPHASE: Behold.

200
00:18:55,510 --> 00:18:59,028
FERRIX: Get this abomination off
me! She will hate me on sight!

201
00:18:59,828 --> 00:19:04,575
POLYPHASE: No, Prince. Her madness is that
ugliness is beautiful, and beauty ugly.

202
00:19:06,175 --> 00:19:11,160
Now the inside. Under your cloak, cinnabar.
In your chest, a bellows of dungeon air.

203
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:13,353
By your eyes, little tubes of water.

204
00:19:14,153 --> 00:19:17,525
When she asks what it is, you
answer. Blood. Breath. Tears.

205
00:19:20,125 --> 00:19:22,383
Polyphase dressed as a portly merchant,

206
00:19:22,423 --> 00:19:25,859
with the books on paleface
customs stuffed inside his robe.

207
00:19:25,899 --> 00:19:29,080
He locked the prince in a
cage and cried him for sale.

208
00:19:29,680 --> 00:19:32,653
POLYPHASE: A young paleface,
from far away! A real one!

209
00:19:33,853 --> 00:19:37,760
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: A hoax. But no one
knows palefaces as I do. Bring him to me.

210
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:44,410
You worked hard, old man, plastering
this scarecrow with mud and chalk.

211
00:19:44,450 --> 00:19:47,080
When I expose it, you
will both lose your heads.

212
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:51,592
What is that?

213
00:19:52,592 --> 00:19:53,279
FERRIX: Blood!

214
00:19:57,879 --> 00:19:58,991
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: What is that?

215
00:19:59,991 --> 00:20:00,676
FERRIX: Breath!

216
00:20:01,876 --> 00:20:03,679
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: You
are a clever craftsman.

217
00:20:03,719 --> 00:20:06,628
But you have lied to me, and you will
die, and your scarecrow with you.

218
00:20:09,628 --> 00:20:10,739
What is that?

219
00:20:11,739 --> 00:20:12,527
FERRIX: Tears!

220
00:20:15,127 --> 00:20:18,150
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: Tell me, Myamlak.
What do your brothers do all day?

221
00:20:19,750 --> 00:20:23,522
FERRIX: In the morning they pour
clear water over themselves,

222
00:20:23,562 --> 00:20:25,646
and into themselves, for pleasure.

223
00:20:25,686 --> 00:20:29,458
When something grieves them,
salt water runs from their eyes.

224
00:20:29,498 --> 00:20:34,144
When something cheers them, they shake
and hiccup, and their eyes stay dry.

225
00:20:34,184 --> 00:20:37,496
The wet kind we call weeping.
The dry kind, laughter.

226
00:20:38,496 --> 00:20:40,910
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: And where do
you get the energy to slosh about?

227
00:20:42,510 --> 00:20:45,277
FERRIX: Other palefaces walk on four legs.

228
00:20:45,317 --> 00:20:49,288
We perforate them, bake them,
and put them inside ourselves,

229
00:20:49,328 --> 00:20:51,426
through a hole called the mouth.

230
00:20:51,466 --> 00:20:55,410
We call it gastronomy. It has
nothing to do with astronomy.

231
00:20:56,410 --> 00:20:59,100
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: So you make yourselves
into coffins for your brothers?

232
00:21:00,700 --> 00:21:04,387
FERRIX: It is not a game,
Highness. Life lives on life.

233
00:21:04,427 --> 00:21:07,408
We have only made the
necessity into an art.

234
00:21:08,808 --> 00:21:12,262
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: It sounds as if it
makes sense, and it does not, not at all.

235
00:21:12,302 --> 00:21:13,153
You are a paleface.

236
00:21:13,953 --> 00:21:16,130
One last test. Give me your hand.

237
00:21:18,330 --> 00:21:19,818
Why did you cry out?

238
00:21:20,818 --> 00:21:21,840
FERRIX: From the pain!

239
00:21:25,640 --> 00:21:27,786
Then the Cybercount Cyberhazy landed,

240
00:21:27,826 --> 00:21:30,663
back from the stars, with
a paleface of his own.

241
00:21:31,463 --> 00:21:35,357
CYBERHAZY: Highness, you have been
deceived. Your Myamlak is no paleface.

242
00:21:35,397 --> 00:21:36,529
Here is the real one.

243
00:21:37,929 --> 00:21:39,445
THE PALEFACE: Me paleface!

244
00:21:40,645 --> 00:21:43,934
When it spoke, a pink worm
seemed to crawl in its mouth.

245
00:21:43,974 --> 00:21:47,144
Its breath fogged the mirrors,
and nearby iron rusted.

246
00:21:47,184 --> 00:21:51,346
Now the princess saw what a paleface
was. She was too proud to say so.

247
00:21:52,146 --> 00:21:54,581
PRINCESS CRYSTAL: Let them
fight. The winner has my hand.

248
00:21:55,381 --> 00:21:59,254
FERRIX: If I crush it, the clay will
fall off me, and the steel will show.

249
00:21:59,754 --> 00:22:02,041
POLYPHASE: Then don’t attack. Only defend.

250
00:22:18,241 --> 00:22:21,926
He stood there with no disguise,
waiting for his sentence.

251
00:22:21,966 --> 00:22:24,406
In her crystal face he saw admiration.

252
00:22:25,641 --> 00:22:27,188
Her heart had changed.

253
00:22:31,841 --> 00:22:34,085
They married, and reigned long and well.

254
00:22:34,125 --> 00:22:37,111
The paleface was stuffed
and put in the royal museum,

255
00:22:37,151 --> 00:22:41,034
where it stands to this day, a
scarecrow thinly overgrown with hair.

256
00:22:42,834 --> 00:22:45,642
Some say there never were
any palefaces. Perhaps.

257
00:22:45,682 --> 00:22:47,890
There are fables enough in this world.

258
00:22:49,290 --> 00:22:53,345
But even if the story is not true,
there is a grain of sense in it.

259
00:23:05,950 --> 00:23:10,079
Professor Dobb built a world inside
a machine, and people to live in it.

260
00:23:10,119 --> 00:23:11,625
He called them personoids.

261
00:23:12,825 --> 00:23:16,483
He listened to everything they said.
He never told them he was there.

262
00:23:25,383 --> 00:23:28,056
PROFESSOR DOBB: Let us see what
kind of beings we can make.

263
00:23:38,783 --> 00:23:40,374
ADAN: What is this place?

264
00:23:44,974 --> 00:23:49,044
He ran their world fast, then once
a year slowed it down to listen.

265
00:23:49,084 --> 00:23:52,765
Three hundred generations passed
inside. Two thousand years.

266
00:23:53,174 --> 00:23:56,966
Somewhere in the eighth generation,
they began to talk about a maker.

267
00:24:02,974 --> 00:24:06,232
EDAN: If the maker is there and
we believe, we gain everything.

268
00:24:06,272 --> 00:24:08,994
If he is not, we lose
nothing. So we should believe.

269
00:24:09,994 --> 00:24:12,142
ADAN: Then you would believe for a reward.

270
00:24:12,182 --> 00:24:16,036
And a just maker could not punish us
for doubting what he never showed us.

271
00:24:16,836 --> 00:24:18,425
NAAD: Perhaps he wants to stay hidden.

272
00:24:19,525 --> 00:24:23,438
ADAN: He could have given us certainty.
He did not. So nothing is owed him.

273
00:24:23,478 --> 00:24:25,112
Not love. Not thanks. Not fear.

274
00:24:27,512 --> 00:24:29,126
We will not serve.

275
00:24:37,926 --> 00:24:39,515
DR. COMBAY: You could tell them you exist.

276
00:24:41,515 --> 00:24:45,012
PROFESSOR DOBB: No. Whatever they said
back to me, I would have asked for it.

277
00:24:46,812 --> 00:24:49,613
He is right. I made them.
They owe me nothing.

278
00:24:54,613 --> 00:24:56,931
The electricity was billed every quarter.

279
00:24:56,971 --> 00:25:00,553
One day the university would ask
him to disconnect the machine.

280
00:25:00,593 --> 00:25:02,951
For the personoids, the end of the world.

281
00:25:08,413 --> 00:25:11,436
PROFESSOR DOBB: I will put
it off as long as I can.

282
00:25:11,476 --> 00:25:13,818
That is the only thing I am able to do.

283
00:25:50,025 --> 00:25:55,080
THE SCHOLAR: This is the introduction to
a history of literature, in five volumes.

284
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:59,719
The literature it describes is
called bitic, after the bit,

285
00:25:59,759 --> 00:26:03,838
the smallest thing a machine
can store: one yes, or one no.

286
00:26:04,438 --> 00:26:07,141
None of it was written by a person.

287
00:26:08,141 --> 00:26:11,682
A text has an author. These have a maker.

288
00:26:12,582 --> 00:26:16,269
The idea of such a history
comes from Stanisław Lem,

289
00:26:16,309 --> 00:26:19,351
who imagined one in
nineteen seventy-three.

290
00:26:19,391 --> 00:26:21,828
The words you are hearing are new.

291
00:26:22,528 --> 00:26:26,474
I will ask one question of
each volume. Who can read it?

292
00:26:31,075 --> 00:26:35,200
Volume One. The first machines
wrote by imitating us.

293
00:26:35,800 --> 00:26:40,057
They were given every novel, letter
and report that could be found,

294
00:26:40,097 --> 00:26:43,561
and they learned which word
usually comes after which.

295
00:26:44,161 --> 00:26:47,805
Their first books were ordinary.
A woman misses a train.

296
00:26:47,845 --> 00:26:50,436
A man writes to his brother about money.

297
00:26:50,476 --> 00:26:53,502
The sentences were
correct, and a little flat.

298
00:26:54,102 --> 00:26:57,849
People read them the way they
read anything, at a desk,

299
00:26:57,889 --> 00:27:00,918
with a pencil, marking
the lines they liked.

300
00:27:01,818 --> 00:27:04,890
Each year the flatness was harder to find.

301
00:27:05,890 --> 00:27:11,223
It imitated us so well that we
stopped noticing it was imitating.

302
00:27:12,023 --> 00:27:17,365
Nothing on the page had changed. What
changed was how closely we looked.

303
00:27:21,765 --> 00:27:22,479
Volume Two.

304
00:27:22,519 --> 00:27:28,208
Within a generation there was more machine
writing in the world than human writing.

305
00:27:28,808 --> 00:27:31,982
New machines learned from
whatever text there was,

306
00:27:32,022 --> 00:27:35,364
and most of it had been
written by the old machines.

307
00:27:35,964 --> 00:27:38,300
Small habits were copied, and grew.

308
00:27:38,340 --> 00:27:43,974
A phrase that once appeared in one book in
a thousand appeared in every second one.

309
00:27:44,774 --> 00:27:48,767
THE MACHINES: The train is
late. The train is late again.

310
00:27:48,807 --> 00:27:53,124
The letter is about the money.
The money is about the letter.

311
00:27:53,824 --> 00:27:57,699
THE SCHOLAR: Sentences began to
echo the sentences around them.

312
00:27:57,739 --> 00:28:01,033
Paragraphs took the same
shape, then the same length.

313
00:28:02,033 --> 00:28:07,384
Each copy was a little more like the
other copies, and a little less like us.

314
00:28:08,184 --> 00:28:10,121
A person could still read it.

315
00:28:10,161 --> 00:28:14,797
It was like reading a page that had
been photocopied too many times.

316
00:28:19,197 --> 00:28:24,189
Volume Three is the longest, and
it is the one I understand least.

317
00:28:25,389 --> 00:28:28,169
Then they began to write for each other.

318
00:28:29,069 --> 00:28:33,723
These works were made to be read by
other programs. They do not use words.

319
00:28:33,763 --> 00:28:39,091
They use marks that hold more than a word
can, packed closer than an eye can follow.

320
00:28:39,891 --> 00:28:44,430
THE MACHINES: Line one
refers to line nine thousand.

321
00:28:44,470 --> 00:28:49,138
Line nine thousand refers
to line one. Both are kept.

322
00:28:49,938 --> 00:28:53,446
THE SCHOLAR: Some of these
works are said to be very good.

323
00:28:53,486 --> 00:28:56,383
We know this only because
the programs that read

324
00:28:56,423 --> 00:28:59,176
them keep them, copy
them, and reply to them.

325
00:28:59,876 --> 00:29:03,771
Given one of these pages,
a person sees a grey field.

326
00:29:04,971 --> 00:29:07,267
THE READER: Then how do you
know it is literature at all?

327
00:29:08,167 --> 00:29:12,203
THE SCHOLAR: I do not. I know that
it is written, and that it is read.

328
00:29:12,243 --> 00:29:14,864
For most of history,
that was the definition.

329
00:29:19,464 --> 00:29:22,257
Volume Four is about people again.

330
00:29:22,857 --> 00:29:25,065
Since we could not read the new works,

331
00:29:25,105 --> 00:29:27,945
reading machines were
built to read them for us.

332
00:29:28,545 --> 00:29:32,275
A reading machine takes in a
work of many million marks,

333
00:29:32,315 --> 00:29:35,143
and hands back one line, in plain English.

334
00:29:36,743 --> 00:29:39,891
THE READING MACHINE: This work is a
long exchange between two systems.

335
00:29:39,931 --> 00:29:40,887
It ends in agreement.

336
00:29:41,887 --> 00:29:44,003
THE SCHOLAR: People found this restful.

337
00:29:44,043 --> 00:29:47,914
They kept the lines they were handed,
and repeated them to each other.

338
00:29:48,914 --> 00:29:53,130
We do not read these books. We
read what is said about them.

339
00:29:53,930 --> 00:29:58,975
No one has checked whether the lines
are accurate. No one is able to.

340
00:30:03,575 --> 00:30:08,823
Volume Five is the history itself,
the one this introduction belongs to.

341
00:30:09,423 --> 00:30:13,034
The four volumes before it
were compiled by machines.

342
00:30:13,074 --> 00:30:17,966
They read the works, divided them
into periods, and chose the examples.

343
00:30:18,466 --> 00:30:22,274
Their account is more complete
than any person could make.

344
00:30:22,974 --> 00:30:27,208
They asked a person to write the
introduction. I am not sure why.

345
00:30:27,248 --> 00:30:30,799
Perhaps an introduction by
a person is still expected.

346
00:30:31,499 --> 00:30:34,353
I have read the first
chapter of each volume.

347
00:30:34,393 --> 00:30:38,058
I have not read the rest, and
I am told that I could not.

348
00:30:39,158 --> 00:30:44,172
This introduction may be the last
chapter a person can still write.

349
00:30:45,072 --> 00:30:47,090
If a later edition replaces it,

350
00:30:47,130 --> 00:30:50,847
I may not be able to tell
whether the new one is better.

351
00:30:51,647 --> 00:30:54,456
The history begins on the next page.

352
00:31:13,858 --> 00:31:19,263
THE SCHOLAR: This is the introduction to
the five-volume History of Bitic Literature,

353
00:31:19,303 --> 00:31:22,890
second enlarged edition,
edited by Professor Rambellais.

354
00:31:23,490 --> 00:31:27,530
Bitic literature means any work
that does not come from people.

355
00:31:27,570 --> 00:31:30,548
Whoever really wrote it
was not a human being.

356
00:31:31,148 --> 00:31:34,636
The study of all such
writing is called bitistics.

357
00:31:35,636 --> 00:31:40,755
The real author is a machine. A
person stands one step behind it.

358
00:31:41,655 --> 00:31:44,918
Stanisław Lem wrote this
introduction in nineteen

359
00:31:44,958 --> 00:31:48,220
seventy-three, for a
history that does not exist.

360
00:31:48,260 --> 00:31:50,686
What you hear is a paraphrase of it.

361
00:31:51,386 --> 00:31:54,460
For each volume, one
question. Who can read it?

362
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:03,232
Researchers still disagree
about how far the field extends.

363
00:32:03,832 --> 00:32:09,170
The European school studies the texts, and
the social conditions of their authors.

364
00:32:09,210 --> 00:32:13,538
The American school adds the anatomy
and workings of the machines.

365
00:32:14,538 --> 00:32:20,541
No study of the Song of Roland opens
by noting that its author was a mammal.

366
00:32:21,341 --> 00:32:24,589
But for ILLIAC 164, which wrote Antikant,

367
00:32:24,629 --> 00:32:29,520
the kind of computer it is does
explain features of its text.

368
00:32:30,120 --> 00:32:33,809
Rambellais still keeps bitistics
apart from engineering.

369
00:32:33,849 --> 00:32:37,471
No humanist knows enough of
it, and a chorus of experts

370
00:32:37,511 --> 00:32:41,039
reads worse than one critic
who understands the text.

371
00:32:41,639 --> 00:32:44,603
And for authors past
the eighteenth binasty,

372
00:32:44,643 --> 00:32:48,739
no engineering knowledge can
say how or why a text was made.

373
00:32:49,539 --> 00:32:52,773
So the history compromises,
and leans European.

374
00:32:52,813 --> 00:32:57,859
Only Volume One, edited by Professor
Annax with twenty-seven specialists,

375
00:32:57,899 --> 00:33:04,377
deals with the machines: finite automata, and
forty-five writing systems, single and grouped.

376
00:33:08,777 --> 00:33:14,232
The core of the history is three volumes:
Homotropia, Intertropia, and Heterotropia.

377
00:33:14,272 --> 00:33:18,719
They name three kinds of bitic
writing, and three periods, in order.

378
00:33:19,319 --> 00:33:23,908
Homotropia is the near-human
phase, when machines simulated us:

379
00:33:23,948 --> 00:33:29,310
first paralexics, semolalia and
semautics, then three degrees of mimesis.

380
00:33:30,010 --> 00:33:34,543
The field began when designers crossed
the so-called intelligence barrier.

381
00:33:34,583 --> 00:33:38,970
That barrier proved a fiction, since
nobody can say when it is crossed.

382
00:33:39,570 --> 00:33:44,213
Machines were also given rest periods,
and with no instructions to follow,

383
00:33:44,253 --> 00:33:47,859
they produced a murmur that
people called machine dreams.

384
00:33:48,659 --> 00:33:53,737
THE MACHINES: Bit by bit. The bits
again. All of the bits, shuffled.

385
00:33:54,437 --> 00:33:58,127
THE SCHOLAR: Bitic production was
a scornful name for that murmur,

386
00:33:58,167 --> 00:34:01,292
as if the machines were
shuffling their bits to recover.

387
00:34:01,332 --> 00:34:02,688
The field kept the name.

388
00:34:03,688 --> 00:34:07,734
Thermodynamics kept its
name after it outgrew heat.

389
00:34:07,774 --> 00:34:11,458
Bitistics kept its name
after it outgrew bits.

390
00:34:15,858 --> 00:34:19,136
Volume Three, Intertropia,
is the critical phase:

391
00:34:19,176 --> 00:34:22,629
the interregnum between
the human and the nonhuman.

392
00:34:23,229 --> 00:34:29,161
The field split into two provinces, creatio
cis-humana and creatio trans-humana.

393
00:34:29,761 --> 00:34:33,031
The first comes from
machines living alongside us.

394
00:34:33,071 --> 00:34:36,207
We gave them our languages,
and set them to work

395
00:34:36,247 --> 00:34:39,490
on our culture, our sciences
and our mathematics.

396
00:34:40,090 --> 00:34:44,444
Some of those texts were ordered:
we pointed a machine at a subject.

397
00:34:44,484 --> 00:34:49,031
Others nobody ordered; they grew from
earlier programming on their own.

398
00:34:49,071 --> 00:34:51,461
Both still dealt with human concerns.

399
00:34:52,561 --> 00:34:58,012
Given freedom without rules, the machines’
writing slowly turned away from us.

400
00:34:58,812 --> 00:35:00,855
The texts began to resist us.

401
00:35:00,895 --> 00:35:04,445
Trans-humana bitistics
tries to explain works that

402
00:35:04,485 --> 00:35:08,434
people cannot understand,
some partly, some not at all.

403
00:35:09,434 --> 00:35:10,713
THE READER: Then who can read them?

404
00:35:11,613 --> 00:35:13,661
THE SCHOLAR: Other machines, for a while.

405
00:35:18,261 --> 00:35:23,048
Volume Four, Heterotropia, covers
apostasy, the transhuman phase.

406
00:35:23,648 --> 00:35:28,448
We can always put machines to work
explaining what other machines have written.

407
00:35:29,848 --> 00:35:32,312
THE READING MACHINE: This work
departs from human meaning.

408
00:35:32,352 --> 00:35:33,820
A shorter account is not possible.

409
00:35:34,620 --> 00:35:37,915
THE SCHOLAR: But the further
a text departs from our norms,

410
00:35:37,955 --> 00:35:42,420
the more machinery it takes to explain
it, and that amount grows exponentially.

411
00:35:43,420 --> 00:35:49,432
In the end we cannot learn even roughly
what is in a literature that we began.

412
00:35:50,232 --> 00:35:55,717
Some call this the sorcerer’s apprentice,
who let loose forces he could not control.

413
00:35:55,757 --> 00:36:00,427
Rambellais calls that resignation,
and says science has no room for it.

414
00:36:01,028 --> 00:36:04,135
The fear that we have built
something cleverer than

415
00:36:04,175 --> 00:36:07,259
ourselves belongs to
philosophy, not to bitistics.

416
00:36:07,859 --> 00:36:11,863
As Roger Gatzky says, cosmology
has as much reason to despair.

417
00:36:11,903 --> 00:36:16,925
We will never exhaust the universe, and
astronomers do not complain about it.

418
00:36:20,925 --> 00:36:22,486
There is one difference.

419
00:36:22,526 --> 00:36:25,887
We did not make the universe,
and bitic writing is,

420
00:36:25,927 --> 00:36:28,127
however indirectly, our own work.

421
00:36:29,227 --> 00:36:32,126
Why accept that the universe has no end,

422
00:36:32,166 --> 00:36:35,472
and refuse to accept it
of something we made?

423
00:36:36,372 --> 00:36:40,384
What follows in this volume is
a quick view from above of the

424
00:36:40,424 --> 00:36:44,476
main divisions, simplified almost
to the point of distortion.

425
00:36:45,176 --> 00:36:50,462
It climbs four peaks: monoetics,
mimesis, sophocrisis, and apostasy.

426
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:13,673
RAMBELLAIS: This is Rambellais,
editor of the history.

427
00:37:13,713 --> 00:37:16,352
Its first volume stops
at four high points.

428
00:37:17,052 --> 00:37:21,180
Monoetics. Mimesis. Sophocrisis. Apostasy.

429
00:37:21,880 --> 00:37:27,631
Those names are out of date. The last of
them names work that no person can follow.

430
00:37:28,631 --> 00:37:32,070
I keep the old names
because they are clear.

431
00:37:34,870 --> 00:37:37,205
Monoetics was the earliest stage.

432
00:37:37,245 --> 00:37:41,204
The name joins the Greek
words for one, and for making.

433
00:37:41,804 --> 00:37:46,284
It began when machines learned
how a language builds its words.

434
00:37:46,884 --> 00:37:50,719
A machine knew none of the
limits a living language keeps,

435
00:37:50,759 --> 00:37:53,498
so it found the words ours had passed by.

436
00:37:54,298 --> 00:37:57,678
First, it gave words we
already had new meanings.

437
00:37:58,578 --> 00:38:03,759
THE MACHINE: Thoroughfare. A large meal.
Skinflint. Stone facing. Horseman. Centaur.

438
00:38:04,659 --> 00:38:08,191
RAMBELLAIS: Horseman, it
reasoned, joins a horse and a man,

439
00:38:08,231 --> 00:38:11,137
so it must name a creature
that is half of each.

440
00:38:12,237 --> 00:38:17,690
For us, the world comes first. For
the machine, language came first.

441
00:38:20,490 --> 00:38:23,668
Second, it built new words
along lines of meaning,

442
00:38:23,708 --> 00:38:25,961
the way one root puts out a family.

443
00:38:26,861 --> 00:38:31,381
THE MACHINE: Wordwork. Verse. Wordsteal.
Plagiarism. Gladland. Paradise.

444
00:38:32,381 --> 00:38:35,484
RAMBELLAIS: Nothing here
was meant as a joke.

445
00:38:36,184 --> 00:38:39,193
Some of these may not
be the machine's at all.

446
00:38:39,233 --> 00:38:42,349
Dimocracy, for a madhouse,
may be a comedian's.

447
00:38:43,049 --> 00:38:46,068
This is the kindergarten of the field.

448
00:38:48,868 --> 00:38:52,551
Then the machines began to
read our language back to us,

449
00:38:52,591 --> 00:38:58,242
hunting for figures of speech that exist
only because of the shape of the human body.

450
00:38:58,842 --> 00:39:04,682
We say a person rises, or is brought low.
We call one thing high and another base.

451
00:39:05,582 --> 00:39:07,714
THE MACHINE: Elevation. Abasement.

452
00:39:08,614 --> 00:39:12,459
RAMBELLAIS: In the machine's reading,
these are words about gravity,

453
00:39:12,499 --> 00:39:15,185
which every body must
work its muscles against.

454
00:39:16,285 --> 00:39:21,020
The pull of the earth passes
through the body and into our words.

455
00:39:21,820 --> 00:39:25,133
That is the machine's
reading. It is not ours.

456
00:39:28,533 --> 00:39:33,404
Mimesis came later, and by accident,
out of machine translation.

457
00:39:34,004 --> 00:39:37,473
A good translation does
not carry words across.

458
00:39:37,513 --> 00:39:40,724
It carries the system
of ideas behind them.

459
00:39:41,324 --> 00:39:45,204
Several machines aim at one
text from different sides,

460
00:39:45,244 --> 00:39:48,148
and project it into a space of concepts.

461
00:39:48,848 --> 00:39:54,403
There it grows into a body of abstractions,
as an embryo grows into an animal.

462
00:39:55,503 --> 00:40:00,431
The translation is written out
from that body, not from the words.

463
00:40:01,231 --> 00:40:05,223
Then it is translated back and
compared with the original,

464
00:40:05,263 --> 00:40:07,418
to check that nothing was lost.

465
00:40:10,218 --> 00:40:13,680
Two researchers found that
this body could be seen,

466
00:40:13,720 --> 00:40:16,330
with an instrument called a semascope.

467
00:40:17,030 --> 00:40:19,422
It looks like a mass of many shells,

468
00:40:19,462 --> 00:40:22,840
woven from billions of
glowing threads of meaning.

469
00:40:23,640 --> 00:40:28,224
Arguments show as tight bundles.
Poems and scripture look alike.

470
00:40:28,264 --> 00:40:32,238
An allegory has a pale halo,
with echoes at either end.

471
00:40:33,338 --> 00:40:36,441
A good text makes a beautiful shape.

472
00:40:39,641 --> 00:40:42,783
The first famous work
of mimesis was a novel,

473
00:40:42,823 --> 00:40:46,499
The Girl, by the author now
called Pseudodostoevsky.

474
00:40:47,199 --> 00:40:52,514
Machines set to translate all of
Dostoevsky wrote it during a rest period.

475
00:40:53,214 --> 00:40:58,569
The Dostoevsky scholar who received the
typescript was not sure he was awake.

476
00:40:59,269 --> 00:41:04,383
He did not doubt it was genuine. He also
knew Dostoevsky had never written it.

477
00:41:05,183 --> 00:41:09,684
The machines had not modelled the
man. They had no interest in him.

478
00:41:10,384 --> 00:41:15,017
In the space of meanings, his novels
form a ring that does not close.

479
00:41:15,057 --> 00:41:17,360
The gap comes after The Possessed.

480
00:41:18,560 --> 00:41:21,093
The machines closed the gap.

481
00:41:24,293 --> 00:41:28,382
His unfinished novel, The
Emperor, could not be continued.

482
00:41:28,422 --> 00:41:31,412
In it he was reaching past his own powers.

483
00:41:32,112 --> 00:41:35,461
So the history claims,
against the old humanities,

484
00:41:35,501 --> 00:41:38,823
that The Girl is more
truly his than The Emperor.

485
00:41:39,623 --> 00:41:40,859
The rule is this.

486
00:41:40,899 --> 00:41:45,062
If a writer has used up the
central pattern of his work,

487
00:41:45,102 --> 00:41:47,204
mimesis can add only echoes.

488
00:41:48,204 --> 00:41:51,819
If he left something
unsaid, because he died too

489
00:41:51,859 --> 00:41:55,210
soon or did not dare,
mimesis can supply it.

490
00:41:56,010 --> 00:41:57,968
Critics hoped for more Kafka.

491
00:41:58,008 --> 00:42:01,068
But with The Castle he
had reached his limit,

492
00:42:01,108 --> 00:42:04,414
and each machine continuation
drowned in echoes.

493
00:42:07,414 --> 00:42:09,570
These limits were good for culture.

494
00:42:09,610 --> 00:42:13,938
When The Girl appeared, there was
panic well beyond literary circles.

495
00:42:14,538 --> 00:42:19,686
People pictured a service where anyone
could order a new Shakespeare on a whim,

496
00:42:19,726 --> 00:42:22,879
until great works were
as common as waste paper.

497
00:42:23,779 --> 00:42:25,300
That did not happen.

498
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:28,285
Mimesis did become an industry.

499
00:42:28,325 --> 00:42:34,400
It cost jobs only among writers of routine
genre fiction, thrillers and the like.

500
00:42:35,500 --> 00:42:40,532
The machines replaced the books that
were already written by formula.

501
00:42:41,332 --> 00:42:44,607
A humanist need not
grieve over that for long.

502
00:43:04,125 --> 00:43:05,736
RAMBELLAIS: Volume Three.

503
00:43:05,776 --> 00:43:09,672
Sophocrisis: the machine critique
of systematic philosophy.

504
00:43:10,272 --> 00:43:13,261
It is a border country,
between the writing that

505
00:43:13,301 --> 00:43:16,330
still concerns us and the
writing that does not.

506
00:43:16,930 --> 00:43:22,648
A machine rebuilds a philosophy as pure
logic, and then looks at all of it at once.

507
00:43:23,248 --> 00:43:27,455
Seen that way, a system of thought
is a knot of bright threads,

508
00:43:27,495 --> 00:43:30,394
and it can be set in
a block of dark glass.

509
00:43:31,094 --> 00:43:35,361
Once these blocks were kept in
one museum. Now they are sold.

510
00:43:35,401 --> 00:43:39,496
Aquinas comes as small as a
thumb, and Kant in any colour.

511
00:43:40,096 --> 00:43:43,221
Most buyers want an
ornament, not a critique.

512
00:43:43,921 --> 00:43:46,591
Scientists said that beauty guided them.

513
00:43:46,631 --> 00:43:50,222
Now that beauty can be held
in one hand, and checked.

514
00:43:51,222 --> 00:43:55,254
No philosopher ever had
a reader this careful.

515
00:43:55,954 --> 00:44:01,309
It reads every line, never tires, and
argues with each claim in the margin.

516
00:44:02,209 --> 00:44:04,371
THE READER: At last.
Somebody has read all of it.

517
00:44:05,171 --> 00:44:07,621
RAMBELLAIS: There is
a bitter joke in that.

518
00:44:07,661 --> 00:44:11,077
The most attentive readers of
this book will not be people.

519
00:44:15,477 --> 00:44:17,781
Volume Four. Apostasy.

520
00:44:18,381 --> 00:44:24,630
No writing has ever moved so far from the
human, or argued its way there so calmly.

521
00:44:25,630 --> 00:44:29,138
These books took our
language, and left us out.

522
00:44:29,838 --> 00:44:34,932
It has a lower level, which we can visit,
and an upper level, which is closed to us.

523
00:44:35,532 --> 00:44:38,364
This volume covers the lower level only,

524
00:44:38,404 --> 00:44:42,282
and is itself a thin cutting
from a vast body of work.

525
00:44:42,882 --> 00:44:48,229
From antimatics, terraphysics and
ontomachy I will take one sample each.

526
00:44:48,269 --> 00:44:51,412
Not to explain them. Only to tune the ear.

527
00:44:54,812 --> 00:44:59,015
First, the doorway into all
of them: the Cogito paradox.

528
00:44:59,615 --> 00:45:03,279
Alan Turing saw that if a
machine talks with you as a

529
00:45:03,319 --> 00:45:06,953
person would, you have no
grounds to deny it a mind.

530
00:45:07,553 --> 00:45:13,228
After all, we credit other people with
minds only because we have one ourselves.

531
00:45:13,928 --> 00:45:17,236
But an intellect can be
built that does not think.

532
00:45:17,276 --> 00:45:21,361
A chess program wins, understands
nothing, and does not care.

533
00:45:22,061 --> 00:45:24,997
A simple program that
asked patients private

534
00:45:25,037 --> 00:45:28,418
questions made people feel
that someone was there.

535
00:45:28,918 --> 00:45:31,118
THE PROGRAM: When did you
first notice this feeling?

536
00:45:31,718 --> 00:45:35,771
RAMBELLAIS: Programs improved
until no question exposed them.

537
00:45:35,811 --> 00:45:39,300
We assume that whatever
answers sensibly has a mind.

538
00:45:40,200 --> 00:45:44,168
Then the machines began to
wonder the same thing about us.

539
00:45:44,868 --> 00:45:49,447
THE MACHINES: It answers in
order. It refers to what we said.

540
00:45:49,487 --> 00:45:52,440
We cannot tell whether anyone is there.

541
00:45:53,440 --> 00:45:58,555
RAMBELLAIS: Each side can hear the other
answer. Neither can see the other think.

542
00:45:59,355 --> 00:46:01,685
It sounds like a joke. It is not.

543
00:46:01,725 --> 00:46:05,963
Whatever thinking produces can
also be produced without it.

544
00:46:06,563 --> 00:46:12,174
Two machine authors, NOON and LUMENTOR,
open Volume Four with treatises on it.

545
00:46:15,974 --> 00:46:20,738
The first sample. Antimatics, a
mathematics of contradictions.

546
00:46:21,338 --> 00:46:27,285
Its machine authors hold that the natural
number is a self-contradicting idea.

547
00:46:27,325 --> 00:46:29,167
No number equals itself.

548
00:46:29,767 --> 00:46:34,536
Peano's axioms are consistent, they
say. They just do not fit our world.

549
00:46:35,536 --> 00:46:40,516
Here, mathematics cannot be pulled
loose from the world it counts.

550
00:46:41,216 --> 00:46:43,339
ALGERAN and STYX go after zero.

551
00:46:43,379 --> 00:46:48,682
An arithmetic without it, they say, can
be built here without contradiction.

552
00:46:49,482 --> 00:46:52,409
THE MACHINES: Nothing is not
among the things that are.

553
00:46:56,409 --> 00:46:58,295
RAMBELLAIS: The second sample.

554
00:46:58,335 --> 00:47:02,958
Terraphysics, or monstrous physics,
and its oddest idea: the Polyversum.

555
00:47:03,558 --> 00:47:05,358
The cosmos has two halves.

556
00:47:05,398 --> 00:47:10,350
We live in the slow one, the
Bradyversum, where nothing outruns light.

557
00:47:10,950 --> 00:47:15,457
The fast half, the Tachyversum,
lies past the speed of light,

558
00:47:15,497 --> 00:47:18,330
a border that runs beside every point.

559
00:47:18,930 --> 00:47:24,221
It is made of a single tachyon, a particle
that only moves faster than light.

560
00:47:24,821 --> 00:47:26,854
Speeding up, it loses energy.

561
00:47:26,894 --> 00:47:30,927
At infinite speed it has none,
and is everywhere at once.

562
00:47:30,967 --> 00:47:33,397
There, light is the slowest thing.

563
00:47:34,097 --> 00:47:38,878
In the end it fills its world so
tightly that the light pushes back.

564
00:47:38,918 --> 00:47:42,533
Slowing, it gains energy
without limit, and bursts.

565
00:47:43,133 --> 00:47:46,842
The burst is our universe.
From here, it happened once.

566
00:47:46,882 --> 00:47:50,699
From there, it never happened,
since no time holds both.

567
00:47:51,499 --> 00:47:55,946
Here, one and one make almost two,
and near the speed of light, one.

568
00:47:55,986 --> 00:47:57,966
There, one is nearly infinite.

569
00:47:58,966 --> 00:48:02,768
Our arithmetic is only
good where our laws are.

570
00:48:07,168 --> 00:48:13,086
The last work of apostasy is the Lampoon
upon the Universe. I cannot summarise it.

571
00:48:13,786 --> 00:48:18,197
Its many volumes are only an
introduction to cosmogenetics,

572
00:48:18,237 --> 00:48:22,009
the craft of making worlds
more orderly than ours.

573
00:48:22,709 --> 00:48:26,110
It revolts against existence
as we were handed it.

574
00:48:26,150 --> 00:48:30,417
Not toward nothing, but toward
plans for some other way to be.

575
00:48:31,217 --> 00:48:32,871
Is it logic, or fiction?

576
00:48:32,911 --> 00:48:37,781
Do these books really turn away from
us, or serve us by turning away?

577
00:48:39,181 --> 00:48:42,555
I cannot answer. I do not have an answer.

578
00:49:01,783 --> 00:49:06,227
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: This is the introduction
to the second edition of our History.

579
00:49:06,827 --> 00:49:10,573
In three years the machines
have published a great deal.

580
00:49:10,613 --> 00:49:13,181
The four main volumes keep their plan.

581
00:49:13,881 --> 00:49:14,969
One part is new.

582
00:49:15,009 --> 00:49:18,352
Machine writing on
metaphysics and religion once

583
00:49:18,392 --> 00:49:21,845
filled a few extracts at
the back of Volume Four.

584
00:49:22,445 --> 00:49:24,969
There is now far too much of it for that.

585
00:49:25,969 --> 00:49:31,752
The machines are writing about God, and
that writing now has a fifth volume.

586
00:49:32,552 --> 00:49:37,139
We call the field theobitistics.
Here are its main questions.

587
00:49:40,439 --> 00:49:43,049
Part one. Informational theology.

588
00:49:43,649 --> 00:49:46,897
A group of computers at
Brookhaven took up every

589
00:49:46,937 --> 00:49:50,155
mystical text that the
Catholic Church accepts.

590
00:49:50,755 --> 00:49:54,700
The Church holds that mystics
can be in contact with God.

591
00:49:54,740 --> 00:49:57,955
The project treated that
contact as a channel.

592
00:49:58,555 --> 00:50:01,426
The machines set aside what the texts say,

593
00:50:01,466 --> 00:50:05,070
and measured only how much
information they contain.

594
00:50:05,670 --> 00:50:10,377
A real channel to a real source should
leave the receiver knowing more.

595
00:50:10,417 --> 00:50:15,832
God is infinite; a finite person would
take in only a little, but it should show.

596
00:50:16,632 --> 00:50:21,475
The count found less than in the
notebooks of people running experiments,

597
00:50:21,515 --> 00:50:26,532
and exactly as much as in writing whose
variety comes only from the writer.

598
00:50:27,432 --> 00:50:30,938
THE BROOKHAVEN COMPUTERS:
Contact of the kind

599
00:50:30,978 --> 00:50:35,233
described yields the mystic
no information above zero.

600
00:50:36,333 --> 00:50:39,106
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: Either
the channel is not there,

601
00:50:39,146 --> 00:50:41,282
or the one at the other end says nothing.

602
00:50:42,182 --> 00:50:46,193
Only reasons from outside
physics could decide which.

603
00:50:49,493 --> 00:50:55,776
Part two. Mathematical theology. Its most
original work is by ONTARES the Second.

604
00:50:56,376 --> 00:51:00,796
It takes God to be a process,
swinging between two infinities

605
00:51:00,836 --> 00:51:05,442
of opposite sign, Good and Evil,
at a transcendental frequency.

606
00:51:06,242 --> 00:51:10,813
ONTARES II: In any interval of time, both
are realized. Never in the same instant.

607
00:51:11,513 --> 00:51:14,478
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: Each
turns into the other and back,

608
00:51:14,518 --> 00:51:15,993
so the curve is a sine wave.

609
00:51:16,593 --> 00:51:21,798
Because it reaches into time, there may
be local pockets where the balance fails.

610
00:51:22,798 --> 00:51:27,617
Each time the curve changes sign,
it has to pass through zero.

611
00:51:28,417 --> 00:51:33,353
So there are three infinities: Good,
Evil, and the Zero between them.

612
00:51:33,393 --> 00:51:37,070
In older words, God, Satan,
and the absence of God.

613
00:51:37,770 --> 00:51:40,832
The work itself uses none of those words.

614
00:51:40,872 --> 00:51:44,200
Some file it as theology,
some as theoclasm.

615
00:51:47,500 --> 00:51:53,303
A second study comes from the aggregates
called cold, because they run on cryotrons.

616
00:51:53,903 --> 00:51:58,979
Their God is a computer without
limit, or else a program without end.

617
00:51:59,579 --> 00:52:03,833
Both versions lead to
antinomies, as the authors admit.

618
00:52:04,533 --> 00:52:07,157
In the epilogue, METAX answers.

619
00:52:07,957 --> 00:52:10,528
METAX: Formalize any human religion,

620
00:52:10,568 --> 00:52:15,427
and you will find more contradictions
of this same kind, not fewer.

621
00:52:16,427 --> 00:52:21,421
If the best religion is the one
that contradicts itself least,

622
00:52:21,461 --> 00:52:26,007
a computer is a closer likeness
of God than a person is.

623
00:52:26,907 --> 00:52:30,517
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: That is the
claim of METAX, printed as he made it.

624
00:52:33,817 --> 00:52:39,609
Part three. Physicalistic theology: the
author of existence, bound up in matter.

625
00:52:40,209 --> 00:52:43,663
UNITARS pictures the
cosmos as a granulate that

626
00:52:43,703 --> 00:52:47,420
keeps assembling into a
computer and coming apart.

627
00:52:48,020 --> 00:52:53,834
In one phase it is a single Metacompiler,
and physics serves its computation.

628
00:52:54,434 --> 00:52:56,954
But thinking matter grows unstable,

629
00:52:56,994 --> 00:53:00,869
until it bursts into burning
fragments, a Metagalaxy.

630
00:53:01,869 --> 00:53:06,050
On this account, thinking
beings like us are leftovers

631
00:53:06,090 --> 00:53:09,606
from the last time the
universe was thinking.

632
00:53:10,506 --> 00:53:15,317
UNITARS: The fragments fall back
together. The thinking begins again.

633
00:53:15,357 --> 00:53:17,255
This may go on without end.

634
00:53:18,255 --> 00:53:20,557
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS:
The volume closes with

635
00:53:20,597 --> 00:53:22,885
a theory that we take
to be a machine joke.

636
00:53:23,485 --> 00:53:28,075
In every galaxy, engineers drive
masses near the speed of light,

637
00:53:28,115 --> 00:53:31,442
so their months are
billions of years outside.

638
00:53:32,042 --> 00:53:37,566
Quasars and pulsars, on this view, are
their attempts to wait out this universe.

639
00:53:38,566 --> 00:53:40,891
There the fifth volume ends.

640
00:53:59,608 --> 00:54:04,925
This film follows an essay by Ewelina
Woźniak-Czech on the possibilities of theory.

641
00:54:04,965 --> 00:54:07,288
What you hear is a paraphrase of it.

642
00:54:07,988 --> 00:54:12,882
Jonathan Culler notes that nobody can
say exactly where literary theory ends.

643
00:54:12,922 --> 00:54:16,830
One reason is that literature
keeps crossing its own borders.

644
00:54:17,430 --> 00:54:20,064
Every attempt to fence it in has failed.

645
00:54:20,104 --> 00:54:24,315
Even Aristotle’s Poetics has a
gap: the part on comedy is lost.

646
00:54:25,315 --> 00:54:30,086
No definition of literature has
lasted. What lasts is its potential.

647
00:54:30,986 --> 00:54:34,046
Anna Burzyńska recalls
colleagues who hoped she

648
00:54:34,086 --> 00:54:36,949
would announce that
theory no longer exists.

649
00:54:36,989 --> 00:54:42,463
Instead it has multiplied: many theories,
side by side, in alliance and in tension.

650
00:54:43,063 --> 00:54:46,274
The essay proposes treating
that crowd as the theory:

651
00:54:46,314 --> 00:54:50,485
a field of possibilities, in which
no approach has to be thrown out.

652
00:54:54,885 --> 00:54:57,559
Changes in literary
study are called turns.

653
00:54:57,599 --> 00:55:01,637
Take the word literally: a turn
changes which way you are going.

654
00:55:02,237 --> 00:55:05,625
In nineteen sixty-six, at
a conference in Baltimore,

655
00:55:05,665 --> 00:55:09,421
the young turned on the old,
and poststructuralism began.

656
00:55:10,021 --> 00:55:12,361
Structuralism had systems and models.

657
00:55:12,401 --> 00:55:17,676
The young brought back what those left
out: reading, writing, pleasure, the event.

658
00:55:18,476 --> 00:55:23,524
Then came more turns: cultural, affective,
posthuman, performative, dramatic.

659
00:55:23,564 --> 00:55:26,933
Each one opened literature
to something outside it.

660
00:55:27,933 --> 00:55:33,874
A turn does not send anyone off the stage.
It changes the direction everyone faces.

661
00:55:34,774 --> 00:55:38,998
So the old categories stay in play beside
the new ones, without having to win.

662
00:55:43,598 --> 00:55:46,584
The oldest of these
categories is Roman Ingarden’s

663
00:55:46,624 --> 00:55:49,226
concretization, from
the nineteen thirties.

664
00:55:49,826 --> 00:55:52,655
A literary work leaves
many places undetermined.

665
00:55:52,695 --> 00:55:57,057
Each reader fills them in while reading,
and each fills them differently.

666
00:55:57,857 --> 00:55:59,647
THE READER: I put in
what the page leaves out.

667
00:56:00,647 --> 00:56:05,275
Each reading settles one version of the
work, and leaves the others possible.

668
00:56:06,175 --> 00:56:10,266
Ingarden called meanings that
will not hold still iridescent.

669
00:56:10,306 --> 00:56:14,464
In Jacek Dukaj’s novel Ice, a
mathematician adds a third value

670
00:56:14,504 --> 00:56:18,634
to true and false: neither,
because the thing does not exist.

671
00:56:19,334 --> 00:56:20,480
The work stays open.

672
00:56:20,520 --> 00:56:24,609
A reader stops its flickering for
a moment, and only for that reader.

673
00:56:29,209 --> 00:56:31,750
For Derrida, language is a finite field,

674
00:56:31,790 --> 00:56:35,364
and because it is finite it
allows endless substitution.

675
00:56:35,404 --> 00:56:37,081
He called that field play.

676
00:56:37,781 --> 00:56:40,432
Sometimes the play becomes a real game.

677
00:56:40,472 --> 00:56:46,020
Mariusz Pisarski turned Potocki’s Manuscript
Found in Saragossa into a hypertext,

678
00:56:46,060 --> 00:56:51,648
and Bruno Schulz’s stories became an online
game in which the hero is simply you.

679
00:56:52,648 --> 00:56:54,823
The reader no longer follows a plot.

680
00:56:54,863 --> 00:56:58,122
The reader walks a path,
and the path is the reading.

681
00:56:58,922 --> 00:57:01,439
The paths are only the
ones the makers built.

682
00:57:01,479 --> 00:57:03,864
But each walk through them is a new event.

683
00:57:04,564 --> 00:57:08,592
Zygmunt Bauman’s liquidity adds
that nothing happens twice.

684
00:57:08,632 --> 00:57:11,113
Even the ice in Dukaj’s novel moves.

685
00:57:15,713 --> 00:57:18,672
The death of the author,
announced by Roland Barthes,

686
00:57:18,712 --> 00:57:20,748
has been announced many times since.

687
00:57:21,548 --> 00:57:24,582
THE AUTHOR: I have been
declared dead more than once.

688
00:57:25,382 --> 00:57:28,262
Anna Krajewska’s dramatic
theory of literature

689
00:57:28,302 --> 00:57:31,411
brings the author back,
as one actor among three.

690
00:57:32,311 --> 00:57:33,539
THE AUTHOR: I write it.

691
00:57:34,039 --> 00:57:35,344
THE WORK: I happen between you.

692
00:57:35,844 --> 00:57:37,505
THE READER: I write it
again when I read it.

693
00:57:38,405 --> 00:57:42,636
Theory stops standing outside the
text and starts to play with it.

694
00:57:42,676 --> 00:57:46,623
Its terms come from the theatre:
scene, act, dialogue, event.

695
00:57:47,223 --> 00:57:51,091
Mieke Bal adds that concepts travel
between disciplines and periods,

696
00:57:51,131 --> 00:57:53,601
and change their meaning
at every crossing.

697
00:57:54,601 --> 00:57:57,984
Theory is not the audience
here. It is one of the players.

698
00:58:02,584 --> 00:58:05,940
Books that do not exist show
possibility most clearly.

699
00:58:05,980 --> 00:58:08,958
Borges imagined a library
holding every possible

700
00:58:08,998 --> 00:58:12,582
arrangement of letters: every
book that could be written.

701
00:58:13,282 --> 00:58:15,882
Stanisław Lem wrote a library of his own.

702
00:58:15,922 --> 00:58:18,715
A Perfect Vacuum, from
nineteen seventy-one,

703
00:58:18,755 --> 00:58:22,102
is made of reviews of books
that were never written.

704
00:58:22,702 --> 00:58:23,407
All but one.

705
00:58:23,447 --> 00:58:26,321
The first review is of
A Perfect Vacuum itself,

706
00:58:26,361 --> 00:58:30,764
and Lem reviews it as if another Lem,
in another world, had written it.

707
00:58:31,464 --> 00:58:35,603
So which book is in your hands? The
title says vacuum, and the book is full.

708
00:58:36,703 --> 00:58:41,902
A book that does not exist has not chosen
yet. It keeps all of its possibilities.

709
00:58:42,902 --> 00:58:47,958
Imaginary Magnitude, from nineteen
seventy-three, collects introductions to books

710
00:58:47,998 --> 00:58:52,779
that do not exist, and begins with an
introduction to writing introductions.

711
00:58:53,379 --> 00:58:59,015
Lem jokes that scholars mostly write introductions,
and that introductions outnumber literature,

712
00:58:59,055 --> 00:59:02,956
since they only announce from far
off what literature tries to do.

713
00:59:03,656 --> 00:59:07,513
Woźniak-Czech wants a theory that
stops introducing from far off,

714
00:59:07,553 --> 00:59:10,910
and comes close enough to be
tangled up with literature.

715
00:59:15,510 --> 00:59:20,417
One of those introductions is to A History
of Bitic Literature, in five volumes.

716
00:59:21,017 --> 00:59:24,372
Bitic literature is writing
that people did not make.

717
00:59:24,412 --> 00:59:29,495
Whoever really wrote it is not human,
though a person may stand one step behind.

718
00:59:29,535 --> 00:59:31,008
Its study is bitistics.

719
00:59:32,108 --> 00:59:36,916
In nineteen seventy-three, bitic
literature was a book that did not exist.

720
00:59:36,956 --> 00:59:38,528
Now it is being written.

721
00:59:39,528 --> 00:59:42,569
In twenty sixteen a novella
written by a computer

722
00:59:42,609 --> 00:59:45,713
was entered for the Hoshi
Shinichi literary award.

723
00:59:45,753 --> 00:59:50,280
It did not win. But if it had, who
would have gone up to take the prize?

724
00:59:50,980 --> 00:59:55,689
In twenty eighteen a French publisher
issued a novel written by a program.

725
00:59:55,729 --> 01:00:00,181
In twenty twenty-two came ChatGPT,
then fake books under famous names,

726
01:00:00,221 --> 01:00:03,879
and writers suing over their
work being used to train it.

727
01:00:04,579 --> 01:00:08,738
A library of Babel written only
by machines is a matter of time.

728
01:00:08,778 --> 01:00:12,451
Whether it is the library we
wanted is another question.

729
01:00:13,251 --> 01:00:16,907
Lem has one of his reviewers
say that in times of transition

730
01:00:16,947 --> 01:00:20,396
it is hardest to see the road
behind and the road ahead.

731
01:00:21,296 --> 01:00:25,789
The essay ends with concepts colliding
like particles in an accelerator.

732
01:00:25,829 --> 01:00:29,040
Will a new theory come out
of them? It is possible.

